help() {
	cat <<"HELP";
NAME

	rrssh - remote recursive ssh

SYNOPSIS

	rrssh [<options>...] <main-command> [--- <rrssh commands>...]

DESCRIPTION

	An automation tool that uses SSH to jump through any number of
	hosts to run commands and transfer files and directories along the
	way.  Its commands are a basic shell-like scripting language
	provided via arguments or a file.  The panic mechanism will stop
	its execution on any unhandled failure in the script, or an
	internal rrssh failure.

	The only requirements for the target hosts are an old bash version
	and basic utilities.

SAFETY CONSIDERATIONS

	rrssh is a bash script that duplicates itself via ssh.  To
	guarentee the termination of all rrssh scripts, its language is
	not Turing-complete and does not allow infinite loops.  The whole
	syntax of the script is validated before execution starts, and the
	syntax is validated by the same code that executes the script to
	ensure identical interpretation of the code.

	In some occasions, such as before and after each rrssh command, a
	message is sent from its current location up to the "root"
	command, which replies with a message to either continue or panic.
	This mechanism (internally called epoll_panic) allows Ctrl-C on
	the "root" command to recursively kill its execution (though not
	before any external command is completed and a new message is
	sent).  This mecanism also forces rrssh to crash if any host in
	the "chain" becomes unreachable for any reason.  Worst case
	scenario would be that rrssh will hang idle infinitely instead of
	dying.  An additionnal crashing mechanism is that stderr is keept
	connected at all times up to the "root" command, and rrssh will
	attempt to write trash data during long operations in case a
	network failure with a parent host eventually leads to receiving a
	SIGPIPE, thus crashing faster.

	TODO: make sure que j'ai bien implémenté les écritures dans fd 2.

	From the beginning, rrssh has a first-class panic system that
	immediately stops its execution when triggered.  The panic system
	was not an afterthought.  rrssh is also built to be highly
	testable, and its test suite is extensive.  For example, the test
	suite performs fault injection to verify that all important error
	paths successfuly trigger the panic mechanism and that no further
	commands are executed.  rrssh includes a missing code coverage
	detection capability to verify that there is a least one test for
	all important error paths.

	To ensure quality, no AI is used to develop rrssh.  It may be used
	only to scan for bugs.

MAIN COMMANDS

	For the main commands that need a script, the script must be
	provided either with the option --- or -f.

	run

		Run the script.

	check

		Examine the script for syntax errors and other problems
		without running it.

	print [-n <index>] [-lt]

		Prints the script, one token per line.  With -n, only print
		that token.  Tokens are indexed at 0.  With -t, only print the
		amount of tokens.  With -l, do not quote the tokens using
		printf %q.

	print-kernel

		Duplicates the kernel to stdout.  Send it to shellcheck for
		analysis.

OPTIONS

	-h, --help

		Show this help message.

	--- <commands>...

		Stop reading options and arguments.  The remaining arguments
		will be interpreted as a script.  --- is necessary if the
		script is provided by arguments.

	-f, --file <filepath>

		Provide a file of commands to execute instead of reading from
		the arguments.  If file is "-", then read from stdin.

		When writing a script file, type words and tokens like you
		would in a shell script.  rrssh parses the file line by line
		with eval so that bash handles all forms of expansions like
		brace expansion, tilde expansion, and command substitution,
		for example.  This way, rrssh can read ANSI-C single quoted
		sequences. If you include a rrssh script in an unquoted here
		document in a standard shell script, use printf %q to properly
		escape all variables inside the here document.  See the
		examples below.

	-p

		Make any command chain that does not exit with 0 trigger a
		rrssh panic.  Default behavior is to only make the parent
		scope exit non-0.

	-i

		Any command host or su that fails will be handled as if their
		internal scope had exited non-0 instead of triggering a panic.

	-n <index>

		Read the description of the command "print".

	-l, -t

		Read the description of the command "print".

EXECUTION

	A rrssh script is usually made of scopes [ ].  Unlike normal
	shells, by default, any command "chain" that exits non-0 will stop
	further execution of the scope and the scope will exit with non-0.
	The -p option will make rrssh panic instead.  By default, any host
	or su command that fails will trigger a panic unless the option -i
	is used.  With -i, a failing host or su command will behave as if
	its inner scope exited non-0.

COMMANDS

	host <destination> [-o <option=value>]... [ <commands>... ]
	host <destination> [-o <option=value>]... : <commands>...
	sudo-host <destination> [-o <option=value>]... [ <commands>... ]
	sudo-host <destination> [-o <option=value>]... : <commands>...

		These commands are the main purpose of using rrssh.
		Duplicates the rrssh kernel through a single ssh connection
		and execute the sub commands on the remote host.  This command
		can include itself arbitrarily many times to reach any depth
		in a network.  The [ ] variants are scoped.  The : variants
		take all the commands until the end of the current scope.  The
		sudo variants will run the actual ssh command through sudo.

	    Options provided using -o will be forwarded to the ssh
	    command.  The following options will always be provided to
	    ssh: ConnectTimeout=10 ServerAliveInterval=15.

		Ex: host appserver1 -o Port=10022 [ --- ls --- ]

	su <user> [ <commands>... ]
	su <user> : <commands>...
	sudo-su <user> [ <commands>... ]
	sudo-su <user> : <commands>...

		Same as the host command, but switches user on the same
		host instead.

	[ <commands>... ]

		Create a subscope and run commands inside.  This is the
		equivalent of a subshell in shell scripting.

	command <system-command>
	command [ <argv>... ]
	--- <argv>... ---

		Call an external command.  Stdout and stderr are currently
		mixed together into rrssh's stdout.  /dev/null will be the
		command's stdin.

	true
	false

		Do nothing, with success or failure.

	print <text>

		Print a line of text to stdout.

	panic <text>

		Trigger rrssh's panic mechanism with the given message.  This
		command makes the execution stop.

	cd <path>
	home
	pushd <path>
	popd

		Change your current directory.

	pwd

		Print the current working directory.

	take-file <path> <new-name> <dropdir>
	take-dir <path> <new-name> <dropdir>

		Bring a file or directory to a drop directory on the current
		host, a parent host, or a future child host.  You can provide
		a new name for the file or directory.  You can preserve the
		current name by providing an empty argument as the name ("").

	drop-dir <name> <path>

		Indicate a drop directory (and its name) to receive files and
		directories taken with take-file or take-dir.  The parent
		directory must already exist.

OPERATORS

	The general syntax of a "command chain" is as follows:

		[try] [not] <command> [(and|or) [not] <command>]...

infix operators

	and
	or

		Run the next command of the chain only if the current exit
		code of the chain is a success or failure.  Behaves exactly
		like && and || in shell scripting.

prefix operators

	not <command>

		Inverses the exit code of the following command.  Behaves like
		preceeding a command with a ! in a shell script.

	try

		Ignore the exit code of the following command chain.  Default
		behavior is when a command chain exits non-0.

OPTIONS USED BY RRSSH'S TEST SUITE

	--make-command-fail <id>

		Do not use this.  This is intended for the test suite.  Makes
		a command fail to validate the behavior of the error paths.

	--fake-su

		Do not use this.  This is intended for the test suite.  Make
		the su commands not really attempt to switch user to prevent
		passwords from breaking the test suite.

COMMANDS USED BY RRSSH'S TEST SUITE

	make-command-fail <id>

		Do not use this.  This is intended for the test suite.  Makes
		a command fail to validate the behavior of the error paths.
		This will be in effect only for the current scope and all
		child scopes.

	kernel-inner-function [--] [<arguments>]...

		Do not use this.  This is intended for the test suite.
		Instead of running the main loop, call an internal kernel
		function, print its output and return its exit code.  This is
		used for helper functions that don't have side effects.

ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES USED FOR DEVELOPMENT

	RRSSH_COVERAGE_FILE

		If defined, file path to append code coverage ids encountered.

EXAMPLES

	TODO

PROBLEMS

	TODO

HELP
}

