# the multiple "operation modes" of the kernel are for scanning or
# other details without having to duplicate code.

# The calling protocol of the kernel never requires the whole user
# script to be provided by arguments because it may be
# "dangerously" long (i dont know whether there is a platform with an
# argument limit) or it may contain credentials (must not show up in
# ps aux).

# The user script, files and directories to transmit, most information
# and other "object" types must be provided at the end of the kernel
# script.  Some basic information like the "operation mode" is
# provided via arguments, though.  The line "###;;;" indicates the end
# of the kernel and the beginning of the objects.  First we put all
# small-ish object, which is anything other than files and folders,
# then a separator, then we dump files and folders.  This is to avoid
# the need to skip large files/folders and thus speed up a little bit.



